+`color_source = "window"` paints the bar with a thin strip of the window's own content instead of `background`, so the bar takes on the application's colour and follows it as the application repaints that edge. `color_source = "average"` paints the bar with that strip's mean colour instead, one uniform colour with none of the window's own detail, so a title bar, tab row or loading bar along that edge does not read as fragments of itself inside the bar. It costs a pixel readback each time the window repaints that edge, which finishes the frame before reading it, so leave it off for windows that repaint that edge every frame, such as video. `color_source_height` sets how many logical pixels that strip takes, from `1` to `4`; its default, `1`, samples the single top row and reads as one flat colour, while a larger value keeps more of the window's own detail. Because the bar is only ever the window's own pixels, anything the application draws along that edge — a toolbar, a tab row, a loading bar — appears in it too. A window whose top edge is transparent keeps the configured `background` there, because the bar colour stays painted underneath, and the values in `[colors.title_bar]` remain the fallback for applications that draw nothing. Unfocused windows keep their own colour, dimmed over `background_unfocused`. Because the bar can be any colour the application paints, the title and the button symbols carry a dark edge, so they stay readable on light and dark windows alike. A client-drawn window that reports its own geometry keeps the transparent shadows around it out of the colour, so the bar follows the visible window rather than its padding. `"window"` mirrors the window's own buffer instead of reading pixels back, so it adds one bar-sized rectangle per window and a copy of that strip each time the window commits. `"average"` does read that strip back, once for each commit that repaints it, which finishes the frame first; the resulting colour is a single pixel, so no