From e63dfe705e90bf26b1317b50d1b8e4754b9bb08a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZhangDM-520 <jixianqiang1540@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:15:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] fix(title_bar): make the average's damage guard real, and
 cut its cost

`pixman_region32_intersect` reports whether the operation succeeded, not
whether the result is non-empty: a disjoint strip and damage region still
returned true, so every commit counted as touching the edge. Average mode
therefore read the strip back and finished the frame on every commit of every
window with the mode on, which is exactly the cost the design promised to
avoid, and `window` mode re-mirrored on commits that changed nothing.

Test emptiness on its own, and stop letting a new client buffer count as new
content for the average: it only needs to read again when the edge's pixels
changed, the strip moved, the mode changed, or an earlier read was deferred.

Also from the same review:
- a preview copies the colour its window resolved instead of reading back per
  card, and a preview never reads back on its own;
- a readback waits for the client's explicit-sync point to be signalled, and
  defers to a later update instead of sampling a frame still on the GPU;
- a failed read keeps the last colour rather than falling back to the palette
  and flickering, and a deferred read is the only state that retries;
- the flat colour is described as the window is, the space its mean was taken
  in, instead of being relabelled sRGB;
- the shared colour-manager helper is left alone, so its other callers keep the
  behaviour they had;
- the frame holds its title and shadow buffers, so a close snapshot is the copy
  it always was instead of a fresh cache lookup and Pango measurement;
- the shadow is clipped like the title it backs, the readback scratch buffer is
  released when the bar leaves average mode, and the mean is clamped.

Pins: a harness phase that repaints only the window body and asserts the
compositor logged no strip readback (fails with the old empty-test: 6 -> 8).

